xt. Note the wider corpus is genuinely split (983 curly / 927 ASCII across all 369 modules) —\n   that pre-existing inconsistency is a separate normalization question, not this slice's problem.\n2. **Ajami blocker at merge time (known, accepted for now).** All Ajami fields are null per Muhammad's\n   instruction. But `validate-content.mjs` requires `gapTeaser.ajami` (line 102) and `useTodayPrompt.ajami`\n   (line 115) to be non-empty strings, and the user's own register rule requires a `useTodayPrompt` on every\n   module. A null-Ajami adult-track module therefore **cannot pass the validator**. Harmless while\n   source-only; must be resolved (Ajami pass, or a validator change) before this subject can ever merge.\n   Needs a Muhammad ruling.\n\nAlso: `CT03` is `isChainLeaf: true` / `chainNext: null` because the validator hard-errors on a `chainNext`\nnaming a non-existent id (lines 87-88), so a dangling `chainNext: \"CT04\"` is invalid. Appending CT04 later\nrequires flipping exactly those two fields on CT03.\n\n**Process failure to carry forward:** Claude pointed `--output-last-message` and the brief's `Report to:` at\nthe same path, so the final message **overwrote Codex's detailed report** — losing the required terminology\ntable (candidate Hausa expressions with justifications and rejected alternatives) and Codex's own overlap and\nengine-inspection notes. `CURRENT_STATE.md` line 20 already recorded this exact lesson from an earlier\ndispatch and it was still repeated. Always give `--report` a path distinct from the brief's `Report to:`.\nSeparately, the brief's `Scope globs:` were written inline after the label instead of on their own lines, so\n`codex-postflight.sh` silently skipped the scope-fence check (WARN, not FAIL) — scope was verified by mtime\ninstead.\n\n## Next Step (current)\n\n`Tunani da Tantancewa M1: SOURCE-ONLY PILOT — awaiting human Hausa and content approval.` Muhammad to rule on\nnaturalness, technical vocabulary, tone, whether each quiz has one clearly best answer, and whether the\nsubject feels practically distinct from Vocational Skills and Social Studies — plus the two open items above.\nNo merge into `app/content.json`, no modules 4-10, no `Falsafa`, no commit.\n\n# 2026-07-25 (part 2) — CT M1 corrected: 4 content fixes + Ajami-aware validator\n\nSlice `2026-07-25-slice-02` (dispatch-codex, Terra/high). All four of Muhammad's content rulings applied to\nthe EXISTING pilot (not regenerated): apostrophes normalized to ASCII U+0027 (0 typographic remain, 21 ASCII);\n`Ƙarshe`→`Kammalawa` in CT02 (13 uses, 0 residual `Ƙarshe`); `Hasashe`→`Zato` in CT03 (16 uses, 0 residual);\nCT01 titleHa → `Shin An Tabbatar da Wannan? Tantance Saƙo Kafin A Yaɗa Shi`. Image-manifest labels/titles\nrealigned. Terminology now locked for this subject: `iƙirari` (claim), `dalili` (reason),\n`kammalawa` (conclusion), `tushen bayani` (source), `tabbataccen bayani` (fact), `ra'ayi` (opinion),\n`zato` (unsupported guess), `tantance` (verify), `hujja` (evidence).\n\n**`app/tools/validate-content.mjs` made Ajami-aware** — the blocker is genuinely resolved. Contract now:\n`ajami_validated===false` → Ajami fields may be null (non-null must still be a non-empty string);\n`ajami_validated===true` → all required Ajami fields present and non-empty; Latin fields, object shapes,\nquiz and chain validation mandatory in both states. The change also ADDED strictness (quiz-array and\nbilingual-object validation), so regression risk cut both ways. Added a `--content <path>` flag so fixtures\ncan be validated without touching the live file. Regression harness at\n`tools/ct-batch/run-validator-regression.mjs` + `tools/ct-batch/validator-fixtures/cases.mjs`.\n\n**Independently verified by Claude:** all 6 regression cases pass, and cases 3/4/5 confirmed to fail for the\n*intended* reason (exactly one error each, message checked verbatim — not merely non-zero exit); live corpus\nstill validates at 369 with identical track/leaf/chain counts; **scratch temp-merge validates at 372 modules,\nexit 0** — proving the CT pilot with null Ajami is now mergeable; new apostrophe assertion negative-tested\nacross 6 field types with injection confirmed each time (an earlier no-op test gave a false pass, caught and\nredone); candidate restored byte-identically after every negative test; all 7 protected files byte-identical;\n`hasashe` confirmed surviving only in `p6-bsci-15` (Kimiyya) in its correct hypothesis sense, unmodified.\n\n**Process repairs both landed.** Detailed report (229 lines, all 12 required sections) at\n`tasks/2026-07-25-slice-02-report.md`, separate from the 9-line final message at\n`tasks/2026-07-25-slice-02-final-message.md` — no collision, the lost report is replaced. `Scope globs:`\n\n===== ../New project/ai-system/projects/ajamix/ACTIVE_TASK.md =====\n# Active Task (as of 2026-07-23): AJAMIX image workstream fully shipped; audio track started, waiting on delivered recordings\n\n**This header supersedes everything below** — the Social Studies merge task below is old history (completed 2026-07-17), kept for its record only. See `CURRENT_STATE.md`'s three \"2026-07-23\" entries for the real current state. Images: all 335 curriculum images generated, QA-accepted, composited with labels (overlay pipeline + pin/legend fallback, dispatch-codex slice `2026-07-23-slice-01`), and promoted into `app/images/` (dispatch-codex slice `2026-07-23-slice-02`) — fully done. Audio: recording packages built for all 359 modules, `ffmpeg`/`ffprobe` installed, delivery-QA tooling built and verified — waiting on the user's voice-talent process to deliver a pilot batch before the next step (pause-timestamp reconciliation + guarded promotion into `app/audio/`) can be built against real files. Full handoff: `handoffs/2026-07-23-2222-handoff.md`.\n\n---\n\n# (historical) Social Studies P3–P6 — LIVE MERGE COMPLETE (369 modules)\n\n## Status\n\n**ALL 60 P3–P6 SOCIAL AND CITIZENSHIP STUDIES MODULES ARE NOW LIVE IN `app/content.json`.** Muhammad explicitly triggered the merge on 2026-07-17. All 4 guarded scripts ran in sequence (P3→P4→P5→P6), each independently verified before the next ran. Live app is at **369 modules**, validator-green, hook-lint clean (only the known pre-existing 36 `kara` baseline warnings, zero new), zero redundancy findings, all 309 pre-existing modules byte-identical to the pre-workstream backup, all 6 engine files untouched.\n\n**This is the completion of the entire P3–P6 Social and Citizenship Studies content workstream**, from M0 preflight (2026-07-14) through this live merge (2026-07-17).\n\n## Merge execution record\n\n| Band | Command | Result | Verified |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| P3 | `node tools/p3-batch/merge-p3-socs.mjs` | 309 → 324 | validator exit 0, backup at `tasks/2026-07-17-pre-p3-socs-merge-content-309.json`, all 15 IDs present |\n| P4 | `node tools/p4-batch/merge-p4-socs.mjs` | 324 → 339 | validator exit 0, backup at `tasks/2026-07-17-pre-p4-socs-merge-content-324.json`, all 15 IDs present |\n| P5 | `node tools/p5-batch/merge-p5-socs.mjs` | 339 → 354 | validator exit 0, backup at `tasks/2026-07-17-pre-p5-socs-merge-content-339.json`, family-planning content guard passed clean, `p5-socs-07` confirmed live |\n| P6 | `node tools/p6-batch/merge-p6-socs.mjs` | 354 → 369 | validator exit 0, backup at `tasks/2026-07-17-pre-p6-socs-merge-content-354.json`, terrorism-term content guard passed clean, `p6-socs-09` title confirmed correct in live file |\n\n## Final full-workstream verification (post all 4 merges, independently run by Claude)\n\n- `node app/tools/validate-content.mjs`: **exit 0**, 369 modules, `track=vocational:10 track=formal:359 isChainLeaf:360 chainNext-set:9`.\n- 369 unique IDs; `p3/p4/p5/p6-socs` = 15 each; `p1/p2-socs` = 30 (untouched, as required).\n- `p6-socs-09` live `titleEn`: \"Safety and Security — Staying Safe as a Citizen\" — confirmed correct, no drift.\n- Hook-lint on the full live 369-module bundle: **0 errors, 36 warnings** — all 36 traced directly to 6 pre-existing Basic Science module indices (`p2-bsci-02`, `p3-bsci-01`, `p3-bsci-03`, `p4-bsci-01`, `p2-bsci-16`, `p3-bsci-16`), matching the project's long-documented accepted `kara` context-dependent baseline. **Zero new warnings or errors from any Social Studies content.**\n- Within-module redundancy on the full live bundle: **0 findings** across all 369 modules.\n- All 309 pre-existing modules confirmed byte-identical to the `pre-p3-socs-merge-content-309.json` backup (the true pre-workstream state) — `JSON.stringify` deep-equality check, not just count/checksum.\n- All non-module top-level fields (`version`, `gradeBands`, `activities`, `glossary`, `schemaVersion`, etc.) confirmed identical to pre-workstream state.\n- All 6 engine files (`app.js`, `quiz-engine.js`, `styles.css`, `index.html`, `sw.js`, `bootstrap.js`) checksum-identical to their M0 baseline — untouched throughout the entire workstream including this merge.\n\n## Rollback material (kept, not deleted)\n\nFour full pre-merge snapshots of `app/content.json`, one before each band's merge, live under `ai-system/projects/ajamix/tasks/`:\n- `2026-07-17-pre-p3-socs-merge-content-309.json` (309 modules — the true pre-workstream state)\n- `2026-07-17-pre-p4-socs-merge-content-324.json`\n- `2026-07-17-pre-p5-socs-merge-content-339.json`\n- `2026-07-17-pre-p6-socs-merge-content-354.json`\n\nEach merge script is idempotent and safe to re-run if ever needed for verification.\n\n## Governing context\n\n- Repository: `/Users/muhammadbamalli/Documents/ajamix`\n- Brief: `docs/ARCHITECT-BRIEF-SOCS-P3-P6.md`\n- Full milestone trail: `tasks/2026-07-14-M0-*` through `tasks/2026-07-17-M5-*`, consolidated review `tasks/2026-07-17-SOCS-P3-P6-consolidated-review.md`, this merge\n\n## Next safe action\n\n**No further Social Studies P3–P6 content work is pending.** Natural next candidates for the project generally (not started, no dispatch pending): audio/image asset production for the newly-live 60 modules (currently placeholder paths only, no assets rendered), or a different curriculum workstream entirely. Await Muhammad's direction.\n\n===== SESSION NOTES (tail) =====\n  scan came back clean, and the two collision-risk modules (FL07 fairness, FL08 honesty) correctly stayed\n  in personal territory per the fresh corpus scan Claude ran before writing the brief.\n\n## 2026-07-25 (part 12) — Dispatch-tooling structural fix + Falsafa packet\n\n- Added a hard preflight guard to `codex-dispatch.sh` rejecting any dispatch where `--report` collides\n  (after realpath normalization) with the brief's own `Report to:` line, or where that line is missing.\n  This targets the exact recurring failure from slices 01 and 13. Full detail in `CURRENT_STATE.md` part 12.\n- While negative-testing the new guard, made a path-arithmetic mistake in the TEST harness (not the guard)\n  that let one throwaway dispatch through with a garbage brief, causing one small live Codex call. Verified\n  no actual damage (checksums clean, no new files, lock released). Lesson: verify destructive-guard logic\n  via pure function calls first, not live invocations with `--skip-*` flags as the only safety net.\n- Built `generate-falsafa-review-packet.mjs` mirroring the CT packet generator; produced and\n  fidelity-verified the FL01-FL10 packet (419/419 strings, 0 missing).\n- Noted a packet-quality weakness (heavily templated assessment/sensitivity/image sections) but did NOT\n  re-dispatch to fix it, per the 85% threshold ruling — the underlying content was already independently\n  verified clean by Claude's own read, so the templating is a prose-quality issue, not a substance gap.\n\n## 2026-07-25 (part 13) — Falsafa correction pass: 5 stops, all real, then clean apply\n\n- Slice `2026-07-25-slice-15` needed five stop-and-report cycles before applying cleanly — every one was a\n  genuine precision defect in Claude's own brief (mislabeled fields, a cross-file count ambiguity, an\n  internal Part B/Part C contradiction, and a real architectural gap where the packet generator hardcoded\n  soon-to-be-renamed terminology). Full detail in `CURRENT_STATE.md` part 13.\n- Standing lesson sharpened again: verifying a string EXISTS in a JSON blob is not the same as verifying\n  WHICH FIELD holds it — this caused two of the five stops. The fix that finally worked was generating\n  occurrence lists mechanically from a script (field path + exact live value) rather than describing\n  locations in prose from memory.\n- Real architectural finding, not just a content bug: a packet generator built to assert presence of\n  specific terms will break the moment those terms are approved for renaming. Resolved narrowly (three\n  specific edits, explicitly scoped) rather than reopening the generator's design.\n- One gate finding reconciled as a linter false positive (`kasa` = \"fail/unable to,\" distinct from `ƙasa` =\n  \"land\") without altering Muhammad's own prescribed wording to satisfy an imperfect rule — same class as\n  the earlier `kudi`/`kwaikwayo` false positive.\n- One residual observation (FL10 Q4's independent \"rufe\" framing) surfaced rather than silently fixed or\n  silently ignored.\n\n## 2026-07-25 (part 14) — Falsafa M1 ruling: meets 85% threshold, holding for final read\n\n- Muhammad accepted the slice-15 correction pass at the 85% threshold. `kasa` confirmed correct as written\n  (linter false positive, queued as a future tooling exception, not a content fix). FL10 Q4's wording ruled\n  acceptable as-is; a small alignment fix is queued as a pre-integration patch only, not a dispatch now.\n- No further editorial/assessment work authorized. Remaining gate is Muhammad's holistic read of the full\n  packet, not further correction cycles.\n\n## 2026-07-25 (part 15) — Falsafa M1 INTEGRATED: 369 -> 379 live modules\n\n- Slice `2026-07-25-slice-16`: applied the queued FL10 Q4 pre-integration patch, built\n  `tools/falsafa-batch/merge-falsafa.mjs` mirroring the project's established guarded-merge pattern\n  (re-derived the 27-key adult-track schema rather than copying the 22-key formal-track reference script),\n  and ran the actual live integration. Full verification detail in `CURRENT_STATE.md` part 16.\n- Added one extra safety layer beyond the reference merge pattern: a religious-sensitivity guard, which\n  Claude negative-tested with two injected violations (both correctly caught, source restored\n  byte-identical, live corpus unaffected throughout).\n- Idempotency proven three independent times. All 369 pre-existing modules and all non-module fields\n  confirmed byte-identical. This closes the Falsafa M1 workstream that began with the original\n  Tunani-da-Tantancewa design critique earlier in this session — full arc: critique -> redesign ->\n  pilot -> full authoring -> multi-round Hausa/assessment correction -> human approval -> guarded\n  integration.\n- `Tunani da Tantancewa` (CT01-CT10) remains source-only and unintegrated; not touched this slice.\n- No commit made. That remains a separate, explicitly-gated action.\n\n## 2026-07-26 (part 16) — Critical Thinking guarded integration complete\n\n`CT01`-`CT10` merged into live `app/content.json`, 379 -> 389 modules, via `tools/ct-batch/merge-ct.mjs`\n(mirrors `merge-falsafa.mjs`). Notable process wrinkle: the dispatch command omitted `--add-dir` for the\nsibling `ai-system` checkout, so Codex's own sandboxed attempt correctly failed with `EPERM` on the backup\nwrite and declined to report success — the safe outcome. The merge actually executed when postflight\nindependently re-ran the acceptance-gate command from an unrestricted shell. Claude fully re-verified the\nlive result from scratch afterward (byte-identical pre-existing modules, exact appended-module match, backup\nparity, idempotency, negative-tested religious guard) before treating it as complete, and wrote the report/\nCURRENT_STATE/PROJECT_PLAN entries directly since Codex's own write-back step never ran. Both Falsafa and\nCritical Thinking are now live; no commit made for either.\n\n## 2026-07-26 (part 17) — Vocational audio player browser-verified, 3 real bugs fixed\n\n8/8 acceptance cases pass. Three genuine app defects found by actually running the app that source\nreview had missed: broken prev/next index math, active-highlight leaking onto nav buttons, and the\nconfirmed misleading last-card highlight. Two out-of-scope findings reported not fixed (offline\ndownload-manager still uses obsolete module-level audio paths — needs its own slice; bounded double\nHEAD fire — cosmetic). Learned that Codex's sandbox blocks socket listen(), so server-backed browser\ntests can't be dispatched and must be run by Claude. Status: IMPLEMENTED AND BROWSER-VERIFIED, nothing\ncommitted, throwaway test MP3s to be removed when real recordings land.\n\n## 2026-07-28 (part 18) — Codex second-opinion review of the offline-download fix (slice-29)\n\nRead-only review slice (`gpt-5.6-sol`, medium, `--sandbox read-only`) of commit `a815245`, dispatched\nspecifically to attack rather than confirm Claude's own account of the fix. Verdict: **partially** — core\nfix correct, three genuine gaps Claude missed. Claude independently re-verified every finding rather than\naccepting the report:\n\n1. **Orphaned legacy cache rows (real, unfixed).** Any `audioCache` row written by the *old* buggy\n   downloader is keyed `audio/<id>.mp3`. Post-fix, `getAudioUrlsForModule` never returns that key, so\n   `deleteAudioForCompletedModules` can never reach it and no other sweep exists (confirmed: the only\n   `store.clear()` is in `putAllRecords`, which covers modules/activities/glossary, not `audioCache`).\n   Orphans are permanent. Practical impact near-nil pre-launch (only 5 real mp3s exist, all gitignored),\n   but there is no migration path.\n2. **Cleanup over-reports success (real, PRE-EXISTING — not introduced by a815245).** In\n   `deleteAudioForCompletedModules`, `deletedBytes`/`count` are computed *before* the delete loop, and each\n   `deleteRecord` swallows its error via `.catch(() => null)`. A failed deletion is still reported as\n   deleted. Claude verified against `a815245^` that this code is byte-identical pre-commit — Codex's report\n   did not distinguish pre-existing from newly-introduced; Claude checked and it is pre-existing.\n3. **Latent schema divergence (real, currently foreclosed).** `getAudioUrlsForModule` accepts *any*\n   non-empty `segments` array; `hasFormalSegments` (app.js:3857) requires exactly `=== 3`. A 2-segment\n   formal module would download 2 segment URLs but play back via the legacy path. Confirmed foreclosed\n   today: `validate-content.mjs:141` hard-requires exactly 3 for every formal module (and for any\n   non-formal module carrying `segments`), so this cannot ship — but the two functions disagree on what\n   \"has segments\" means, which is worth tightening for defense-in-depth.\n\nConfirmed-correct by Codex and independently re-verified by Claude: download-time and playback-time key\nspaces are the *same* strings (no case/slash/prefix/index-base/zero-padding transform anywhere in the\npath); all 359 formal modules carry exactly 3 valid `audio/<id>-01/-02/-03.mp3` paths; legacy\n`module.audioFile` untouched on all 359 vs `a815245^`; vocational resolution unchanged; no other active\nformal-segment call site still reads legacy `module.audioFile`. Claude additionally re-derived the full\nlabel set across the corpus: **1,227 generated download labels, 0 duplicates, 0 collisions** with any\nmodule id or activity id (1,077 formal segments + 150 adult cards — exactly matching the QA worklist).\n\n**Correction to Claude's own earlier reporting:** Claude wrote \"20/20 tests pass\" for\n`qa-delivered-audio.test.mjs`. Codex correctly flagged this as imprecise — Node's runner reports `tests 1`\n(one test file with inline assertions), and the 20 `PASS` lines are the file's own console output, not 20\nregistered test cases. The assertions do all pass; the count phrasing was wrong.\n\n**Process notes.** (a) The `Report to:` absolute-path guard in `codex-dispatch.sh` fired again and caught a\nmissing `--add-dir` before dispatch — third time this session, working as designed. (b) Postflight returned\nFAIL on the scope-fence, but this was a **false positive of Claude's own making**: the brief declared\n`Scope globs: (none)` for a no-edit slice, so the report and `.jsonl` files tripped the fence. Verified\nclean by hand — `git status` shows zero tracked-file changes, `git diff a815245` empty, no new commits.\nLesson for future review-only briefs: declare the report/log paths as scope globs (or as \"Known generated\nside effects\") even when the slice makes no product edits. (c) Under `--sandbox read-only`, Codex could not\nwrite its report to the `--add-dir` path (`writing is blocked by read-only sandbox`) — it correctly\nreported `REPORT_NOT_CREATED` and returned the full report as its last message instead, which\n`--output-last-message` captured to the repo-local path. Read-only slices cannot write reports outside the\nsandbox; point `--report` inside the repo for review slices, or accept last-message capture.\n\nNothing fixed this slice — findings reported to Muhammad for a decision, per the standing gate.\n\n## 2026-07-28 (part 19) — Slice-30: fixed all three defects from the slice-29 review\n\nDispatched Codex (`gpt-5.6-terra`, high, `--sandbox workspace-write`) with a precisely-scoped brief to fix\nthe three gaps found in slice-29's review of `a815245`. Claude independently re-verified everything before\nreporting — did not accept the diff or the neutrality claim on the report's word.\n\n1. **Orphaned legacy cache rows** — fixed with a *narrow* cleanup, not the blanket sweep originally\n   considered and explicitly rejected in the brief: caregiver activities carry their own `activity.audioFile`\n   (app.js:4233) that `getAudioUrlsForModule` never enumerates, so a blanket \"delete anything not in the\n   current URL set\" sweep would have been safe only by accident today and would silently eat activity audio\n   the moment that changes. The actual fix: in `prepareDownloadSession`, reuse the `audioCache` read already\n   happening, delete any cached row whose key is a legacy `module.audioFile` belonging to a module for which\n   `hasFormalSegments()` is true, best-effort. Provably bounded to exactly that set — never an activity, a\n   vocational lesson, or a current segment path.\n2. **Cleanup over-reporting** — `deleteAudioForCompletedModules` now counts/sums bytes only after each\n   `deleteRecord` actually resolves (confirmed `deleteRecord` genuinely rejects on transaction error/abort,\n   so the try/catch is meaningful, not cosmetic), still attempts every URL even after one failure.\n3. **Segments-length divergence** — `getAudioUrlsForModule`'s formal branch now calls the existing\n   `hasFormalSegments()` predicate instead of its own ad-hoc \"non-empty array\" check, so download and\n   playback agree on what counts as segmented; falls through to the legacy single-file path if the\n   predicate is true but filtering yields zero URLs (safety net, unreachable today per the validator).\n\n**Independent verification performed by Claude** (not just re-stated from the report):\n- Read the actual `git diff a815245 -- app/app.js` line-by-line — matches the report's stated diff exactly,\n  only the three declared functions touched.\n- Confirmed `deleteRecord`'s promise genuinely rejects on `transaction.onerror`/`onabort` (app.js:9101),\n  making Fix 2's try/catch a real behavior change, not a no-op.\n- **Re-derived the Fix 3 neutrality proof independently** (did not just trust Codex's self-report that it\n  ran this and deleted the script) — wrote and ran a standalone comparison of the old vs new\n  `getAudioUrlsForModule` logic against the real `content.json`: **0 mismatches across all 389 modules**.\n  Confirmed Codex's throwaway `.slice30-audio-urls.mjs` script does not exist in the working tree.\n- Independently re-ran `node -c app/app.js`, `validate-content.mjs` (389 green), and the full\n  `qa-delivered-audio.test.mjs` suite — all pass.\n\n**Process note (same false-positive class as slice-29, now the second time):** postflight FAILed the scope\nfence again — this time because the brief's `Scope globs:` listed the detailed report and `.jsonl` paths but\nomitted the `--report`/`--output-last-message` capture path (`tasks/2026-07-28-slice-30-lastmsg.md`), which\nis a required output of every dispatch, not an edit. Verified clean by hand: `git diff a815245 --stat` shows\nexactly one file changed (`app/app.js`, the declared scope) plus the expected task artifacts, zero other\ndrift. **Standing lesson for future briefs, review or implementation alike: always include the\n`--report`/last-message path in `Scope globs:` alongside the detailed `Report to:` path** — both are\ndispatch-required outputs, neither is a product edit, and omitting either now reliably trips the fence.\n\nNothing committed — Claude reports back to Muhammad for the commit decision, per the standing gate.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
